.TH UPDATE\-EVERY\-THING 1 "2026" "update-every-thing @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
update\-every\-thing \- run every system update in one pass
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B update\-every\-thing
[\fB\-c\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-with\-nvidia\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Runs the updates a desktop needs, in an order that only refreshes repository
metadata once, and prints a summary of what happened at the end.
.PP
The package manager is detected at startup \(em
.BR dnf ,
.BR yum ,
or
.BR apt\-get
\(em and the right commands are used for that system. The two families need
genuinely different handling, not the same command with a different name, so
they are separate paths.
.SS Steps
.IP 1. 4
Antigravity IDE, via
.BR antigravity\-update (1)
if it is installed. Its version is checked first, so nothing is downloaded when
it is already current, and a machine without the IDE is not offered a
first\-time install in the middle of a bulk update. Without the updater there
is no step at all.
.IP 2. 4
System packages. On rpm systems, one
.B makecache \-\-refresh
that carries the run's single metadata refresh, then an
.B upgrade
from that cache. On deb systems, an
.B update
followed by a
.BR full\-upgrade .
.IP 3. 4
Firmware, through
.BR fwupdmgr (1).
.IP 4. 4
Flatpak applications.
.IP 5. 4
Snap packages, where snap exists.
.IP 6. 4
Ubuntu Pro security status, reported only. It covers the ESM archives a plain
full\-upgrade does not, and whether to enable them is a decision, not something
a bulk update should make for you.
.IP 7. 4
Drivers through
.BR ubuntu\-drivers ,
and only with
.BR \-\-with\-nvidia .
.IP 8. 4
GNOME extensions \(em listed only. The distribution\-packaged ones were already
upgraded in step 2, and running the package manager again for them just
re\-downloads every repository to print "Nothing to do".
.IP 9. 4
Cleanup: autoremove, and clearing the package cache.
.SS NVIDIA
NVIDIA and CUDA packages are left alone by default, because replacing the
driver under a running session can break it.
.PP
On rpm systems this is an
.B \-\-exclude
per installed driver package, by name. It is deliberately not a
.I *nvidia*
glob: that also catches
.BR kmod\-nvidia\-\fIkernel\-version\fR ,
the module built for one specific kernel \(em and a package the solver may not
touch is one it may not erase either. The first time the oldest kernel came up
for retirement, the module built for it had to go and could not, and the whole
transaction failed on "requires kernel\-uname\-r = ..., but none of the
providers can be installed", taking every unrelated update with it. The
per\-kernel modules follow their kernel; only the driver is held.
.PP
apt has no
.BR \-\-exclude ,
so the packages are put on hold for the length of the run and released
afterwards. The kernel\-tied
.BR linux\-modules\-nvidia\-* ,
.B linux\-objects\-*
and
.B linux\-signatures\-*
are never held, for the same reason. Packages that were
.I already
held before the run are left held \(em they are not ours to release \(em and the
holds this run adds are released even if you interrupt it.
.SS Failure handling
Nothing aborts the run. A step that fails is retried a bounded number of times
and then given up on, so the run always reaches the end and you get one report
instead of discovering a failure three commands later. A step whose tool is not
installed \(em Snap, Flatpak, fwupd, antigravity\-update \(em does not appear at
all, neither while the run goes nor in the summary.
.PP
Only failures that could go away on their own are repeated. After a failed
system update the transaction is re\-solved from the cache alone \(em no
network, nothing installed \(em and if the package manager cannot resolve it,
the run stops retrying that step, prints the problem once and records it.
Repeating an unsolvable transaction only scrolls the real error off the screen.
.PP
Only the first attempt refreshes repository metadata. A retry does not need a
second copy of it, and on a slow connection downloading every repository again
is the most expensive part of the attempt.
.PP
Neither stdout nor stderr is redirected. That is deliberate: dnf, apt and
flatpak draw progress on stderr and size it from the terminal, so piping either
stream costs them the terminal and their progress bars come out duplicated and
wrapped.
.PP
The one place the output is changed is the metadata refresh on dnf5, which runs
with
.BR DNF5_FORCE_INTERACTIVE=0 .
dnf5 fetches every repository in parallel and redraws a line for each one in
flight; once that block is taller than the terminal window, every redraw leaves
copies of its top lines in the scrollback. Without the live redraw each
repository is printed once, when it is done. The package downloads that follow
keep their live progress.
.PP
The sudo timestamp is refreshed in the background for the length of the run, so
no password prompt appears in the middle of a long download.
.PP
Ctrl\-C stops the run and still prints the summary, marking the step that was
running as interrupted.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-retries " " \fIN\fR
Attempt each failing step up to \fIN\fR times before giving up on it and moving
to the next. Default \fB5\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-with\-nvidia
Update NVIDIA and CUDA packages too.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a usage summary and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every step that ran succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
At least one step failed.
.TP
.B 2
Bad usage, or no supported package manager was found.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted with Ctrl\-C.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.EX
update\-every\-thing
update\-every\-thing \-c 3
update\-every\-thing \-\-with\-nvidia
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dnf (8),
.BR apt\-get (8),
.BR flatpak (1),
.BR snap (8),
.BR fwupdmgr (1),
.BR antigravity\-update (1)
